# GeoHub — URL map
#
# ── How Cloudflare Pages behaves before any rule here runs ─────────────────
#   · a file foo.html is served at BOTH /foo and /foo.html
#   · a request for /foo.html is automatically 301'd to /foo
#   · a request for /index.html is automatically 301'd to /
#
# That second line caused an outage once already. A rule shaped like
#       /feed        /feed.html      301
# loops forever: /feed → /feed.html → (Cloudflare) → /feed → …
# Cloudflare already serves /feed from feed.html, so such a rule is not merely
# harmful, it is unnecessary. Two rules follow:
#   1. never point a rule at a .html target — write the extensionless path
#   2. list both /x and /x.html as sources, since Cloudflare serves the
#      extensionless form directly and a rule matching only /x.html misses it
#
# Cloudflare Pages serves /404.html for anything unmatched, and a missing
# hashed asset must 404 rather than fall through to HTML — so no SPA catch-all.

# ── Retired pages ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only pages that are verifiably dead are redirected: their entry script is the
# 140-byte no-op stub, nothing else renders into them, and their <body> holds
# almost no markup. Each target is a working page.
